## Phase 3–4: The Latency Problem

### What was happening

Original design: single-threaded ESP32 main loop:
1. Sensor POST every 500ms (blocking HTTP)
2. Command poll every 500ms (blocking HTTP, same thread)
3. `time.ticks_ms()` captured **once** at top of loop

**The critical flaw:** Both checks used the same `now` timestamp. When sensor POST was due AND command poll was due in the same iteration, both fired back-to-back. Each `urequests` call on a home WiFi network takes **200ms–3000ms** (variable). Worst case:

```
t=0:    sensor POST fires  → blocks 2s (slow WiFi)
t=2000: command poll fires → blocks 2s (another slow call)
t=4000: next loop iteration begins
```

A command queued at t=500 would not be executed until t=4000 → **3.5 second delay**. Observed range: 5–10 seconds (two 2.5s calls back to back, plus interval).

### Solutions considered

| Option | Why considered | Why rejected/chosen |
|--------|---------------|---------------------|
| `uasyncio` | Single-thread cooperative, designed for MCUs | `urequests` uses blocking sockets — async doesn't help unless you also rewrite the HTTP client from scratch. More complex, higher RAM |
| `_thread` (separate command poll) | Same pattern as radar thread, already proven | ✅ **Chosen** — simple, no HTTP rewrite, works with existing `urequests`, ESP32 dual-core handles it cleanly |
| Faster poll interval | Simple change | Won't help if calls stack on same thread |
| HTTP/2 or persistent connection | True keep-alive reduces per-request overhead | `urequests` doesn't support keep-alive. Would need raw socket implementation |

### Solution implemented: 3-thread architecture

```
Thread 1 (radar.py)    — servo + distance, always running, no HTTP
Thread 2 (_cmd_thread) — GET /command/pending every 300ms
                         COMPLETELY INDEPENDENT of sensor POST timing
Main loop              — POST /sensor-data every 400ms
                         Only ONE HTTP call per iteration (continue after each)
                         Health ping every 250s (keeps Render.com alive)
```

**Key rule in main loop:** `continue` after every HTTP call ensures only ONE blocking call per iteration. After each call, `_last_x = time.ticks_ms()` captures the ACTUAL completion time (not the stale `now` from the top).

**Result:** Command poll executes every `300ms + HTTP_call_time` regardless of sensor POST. Observed LED response time dropped from 5–10s to **500ms–1.5s** (one HTTP round trip).

### HTTP timeout reduction

Reduced `urequests` timeout from `5s` → `2s`. Worst case unresponsive-server delay dropped from 10s (two 5s timeouts) to 4s (two 2s timeouts), and now further to ~2s (only one call at a time).

